---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteChrome from '../components/SiteChrome.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

/*
 * The studio's terms — one document for every DeftDay app.
 *
 * Each app had, at most, a short page of its own. That leaves gaps the moment
 * an app sells something or stores something, and a gap is what gets an app
 * pulled. This covers all of them: who we are, what we sell and who sells it,
 * whose content you are watching, what we do not promise, and what you keep
 * whatever this page says.
 *
 * Written to be read. A wall of boilerplate is not protection — an unfair or
 * unreadable term is void under EU and Turkish consumer law, so overreaching
 * costs more than it buys. Where the law gives someone a right, this says so
 * plainly rather than trying to sign it away.
 */
const updated = 'August 9, 2026';
const apps = 'Munchview, EatStep, and the other apps published by DeftDay Studio';
---

<Base
  title="Terms of Use — DeftDay Studio"
  description="The terms that apply to every DeftDay app: what you may do with them, who sells subscriptions, whose content you are watching, and the rights you keep whatever this page says."
>
  <SiteChrome />

  <main class="legal" id="main">
    <p class="eyebrow">DeftDay Studio</p>
    <h1>Terms of Use</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      These terms cover {apps}, and this website. They are written to be read:
      if something here is unclear, write to us and we will say what it means.
    </p>
    <p class="updated">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <h2>1. Who you are agreeing with</h2>
    <p>
      DeftDay Studio is run by Batuhan Saygın, in İzmir, Türkiye. Our contact
      address for anything in this document is
      <a href="mailto:legal@deftday.com">legal@deftday.com</a>. We publish our
      apps as a trader, and our full identity and contact details are shown on
      our developer page in each app store.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Using our apps</h2>
    <p>
      You may use our apps on any device you own or control, for as long as you
      like. You may not resell them, rent them out, or present them as your own
      work; you may not take them apart to rebuild them as a competing product,
      except to the extent the law expressly allows regardless of this
      sentence. You may not use them to break the law or someone else's rights.
    </p>
    <p>
      You need to be old enough to agree to terms where you live. If you are
      not, a parent or guardian has to agree on your behalf.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Content that is not ours</h2>
    <p>
      Some of our apps show or play material from other services — YouTube,
      Dailymotion, Vimeo, Twitch, PeerTube instances, the Internet Archive, and
      others. That material belongs to whoever made it or to the platform
      hosting it. We do not host it, edit it, choose it on their behalf, or
      claim any right over it, and playing it inside our app does not change
      whose it is.
    </p>
    <p>
      When a platform's own player is used, that platform's terms and privacy
      policy apply to what happens inside it — including
      <a href="https://www.youtube.com/t/terms" rel="noopener">YouTube's Terms of Service</a>
      and, for anything Google does with the data,
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Google's Privacy Policy</a>.
      Advertising shown by those players is theirs, not ours; we neither insert
      it nor take a share of it.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you believe something reachable through one of our apps infringes your
      rights, tell us at <a href="mailto:legal@deftday.com">legal@deftday.com</a>
      and we will act on it and pass it to the platform that hosts it.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Paid features</h2>
    <p>
      Some apps offer a paid tier. <strong>You buy it from the app store, not
      from us</strong> — Google Play or the Apple App Store is the seller of
      record. They take the payment, issue the receipt, and hold the
      subscription. That means:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Subscriptions renew automatically until you cancel them, and you cancel
        them in the store, not in our app. Cancelling stops the next renewal;
        it does not shorten the period you have already paid for.
      </li>
      <li>
        Refunds follow the store's policy, and the store decides them. We will
        support a request you make to them, but we cannot issue a refund for a
        purchase we did not take.
      </li>
      <li>
        Prices are shown in your own currency and include the taxes the store
        applies where you live. We may change prices for future periods; a
        change never applies to a period you have already paid for, and the
        store notifies you of a subscription price change before it takes
        effect.
      </li>
      <li>
        A one-off purchase (where an app offers one) is exactly that: paid
        once, kept, no renewal.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="callout">
      <strong>If you are a consumer in the EU, the EEA, the UK or Türkiye</strong>
      you have a statutory right to withdraw from a distance purchase within 14
      days. Where digital content or a service starts immediately at your
      request and you acknowledge that, that right can end early — which is how
      the app stores present it at the moment of purchase. Nothing in this
      section removes a right the law gives you.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Accounts and your data</h2>
    <p>
      Most of what our apps do needs no account at all. Where an app does let
      you sign in, you can delete that account and everything stored for it
      from inside the app, permanently — and if you would rather not open the
      app, from a page on this site. Deleting is deleting: we do not keep a
      shadow copy.
    </p>
    <p>
      What each app collects, and what it does not, is set out in our
      <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a>, which is part of these terms. The
      short version, true of every app we publish: no advertising, no
      analytics, no tracking, and nothing sold or shared with anyone.
    </p>

    <h2>6. What we do not promise</h2>
    <p>
      We build these apps carefully and we use them ourselves, but we offer
      them as they are. We do not promise that an app will be available without
      interruption, that a third-party platform will keep serving the content
      it serves today, or that an app will suit a particular purpose you have
      in mind. Suggestions an app makes — what to watch, how long something
      takes — are suggestions, not advice.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing in our apps is medical, financial, legal or professional advice.
      Where an app helps you track something about yourself, it is a tool for
      your own use and not a diagnosis.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Liability</h2>
    <p>
      To the extent the law allows, we are not liable for indirect or
      consequential loss, for lost data you had another way of keeping, or for
      what a third-party platform does inside its own player. Where we are
      liable, our liability is limited to what you paid us for the app in the
      twelve months before the claim.
    </p>
    <p class="callout">
      <strong>What we never limit:</strong> liability for death or personal
      injury caused by our negligence, for fraud, or for anything else the law
      of your country does not allow to be limited. If you are a consumer, your
      statutory rights stand whatever this page says, and where a term here
      conflicts with them, they win.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Changes, and ending</h2>
    <p>
      We may update an app, change what it does, or stop publishing it. If we
      discontinue an app you have paid for, we will say so in advance through
      the store and, where a paid period is still running, work with the store
      on a fair outcome.
    </p>
    <p>
      We may update these terms — to follow a change in the law, or because an
      app now does something these terms do not cover. The date at the top
      always says when. If a change materially affects you, we will say so in
      the app or on this site rather than quietly swapping the page. You can
      stop agreeing at any time by uninstalling the app.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Law, and where a dispute goes</h2>
    <p>
      These terms are governed by Turkish law. If you are a consumer, this does
      not take away the protection of the mandatory law of the country you live
      in, and you may bring a claim in your own local courts.
    </p>
    <p>
      Consumers in Türkiye may apply to the Consumer Arbitration Committee
      (<i lang="tr">Tüketici Hakem Heyeti</i>) or the Consumer Court where they
      live. Consumers in the EU may use the European Commission's online
      dispute resolution platform. We would much rather hear from you first:
      <a href="mailto:legal@deftday.com">legal@deftday.com</a>, and we answer.
    </p>

    <h2>10. The rest</h2>
    <p>
      If one part of these terms turns out to be unenforceable, the rest still
      stands. Not enforcing something once does not mean giving it up. These
      terms, with the Privacy Policy, are the whole agreement between us about
      our apps.
    </p>

    <p class="sign">
      Written in plain language on purpose. If any of it reads as a trick, that
      was not the intent — tell us and we will fix the wording.
    </p>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .legal {
    width: min(72ch, calc(100% - var(--gutter) * 2));
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 140px 0 80px;
  }
  .eyebrow {
    color: var(--muted);
    letter-spacing: 3px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .legal h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 3rem);
    margin: 8px 0 16px;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--fg);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .updated {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    margin-top: 8px;
  }
  .legal h2 {
    margin: 48px 0 12px;
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }
  .legal p,
  .legal li {
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.7;
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
  .legal p + p {
    margin-top: 12px;
  }
  .legal ul {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2em;
    display: grid;
    gap: 10px;
  }
  .legal strong {
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .legal a {
    color: var(--fg);
  }
  .callout {
    margin-top: 16px;
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 14px;
  }
  .sign {
    margin-top: 48px;
    padding-top: 24px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
</style>
